      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Lucas reagent reacts by an SN1 path through a carbocation. A tertiary alcohol such as 2-methylpropan-2-ol forms the most stable carbocation, so it gives turbidity immediately. Secondary alcohols react within about 5 minutes, and primary alcohols do not react at room temperature."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t00_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Heated Cu at 573 K dehydrogenates secondary alcohols to ketones. Butan-2-ol is the only secondary alcohol among the options, and it gives butanone."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t00_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Immediate turbidity means X is tertiary, so X is 2-methylpropan-2-ol. A tertiary alcohol has no α-H for dehydrogenation, so over Cu at 573 K it undergoes dehydration instead and gives 2-methylpropene."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t00_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is true. R is also true and explains A: the stable 3° carbocation leads to fast chloride formation, and the chloride is insoluble, which causes the turbidity."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t00_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Statement I is correct because PCC stops the oxidation at the aldehyde. Statement II is wrong because a tertiary alcohol has no H on the carbinol carbon and so resists mild oxidation."
    },
