        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Phenol (pKa about 10) is a weaker acid than carbonic acid (pKa about 6.4). So it cannot decompose NaHCO3. It does react with Na metal, NaOH and KOH."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t02_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "pKa values: p-nitrophenol 7.15, o-nitrophenol 7.2 (lowered by intramolecular H-bonding), m-nitrophenol 8.4 (-I effect only), phenol 10. Decreasing acidity is p > o > m > phenol."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t02_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "The CH3 group in p-cresol is electron-donating (+I and hyperconjugation), which destabilises the phenoxide ion. So p-cresol is less acidic than phenol, while the nitrophenols are more acidic."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t02_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is true: phenol (pKa about 10) is more acidic than ethanol (pKa about 16). R is false and reversed: phenol's resonance involves charge separation, whereas phenoxide delocalises its negative charge with no charge separation, so delocalisation is more effective in phenoxide."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t02_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Statement I is wrong: o-nitrophenol is slightly less acidic than p-nitrophenol because intramolecular H-bonding holds the O-H proton. Statement II is wrong: a meta nitro group acts only through -I, so the para isomer (-R and -I) is more acidic."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t03_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "With bromine water, phenol undergoes trisubstitution because the -OH (and phenoxide) group strongly activates the ring. The product is 2,4,6-tribromophenol, which forms as a white precipitate."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t03_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "o-Nitrophenol has intramolecular H-bonding, so it does not associate with other molecules and is steam volatile. p-Nitrophenol has intermolecular H-bonding, so it is less volatile. The ortho isomer therefore distils over."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t03_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Sulphonation is reversible. At higher temperature (about 373 K) the thermodynamically more stable para product, p-phenolsulphonic acid, is the major product."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t03_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A: Br2 in CS2 at low temperature gives mainly p-bromophenol (II). B: bromine water gives 2,4,6-tribromophenol (III). C: dilute HNO3 at 298 K gives a mixture of o- and p-nitrophenol (IV). D: conc. HNO3 gives picric acid (I)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t03_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is true: -OH activates the ring and directs ortho/para by +R. B is false: phenol is reactive enough that no Lewis acid is needed. C is true: phenoxide forms in water and is more activating, giving tribromination. D is true: per NCERT, sulphonation then nitration gives a better yield because direct nitration causes oxidation. E is false: near room temperature the ortho isomer is the main product. So A, C and D are correct."
    },
    {
