      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "The O atom is sp3, but repulsion between the bulky methyl groups widens the angle beyond 109.5°, to about 111.7°."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t05_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "The Ar–O bond has partial double-bond character, so it is not cleaved. Iodide attacks the methyl carbon (SN2), giving phenol and CH3I."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t05_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Ethers have no O–H bond, so they cannot H-bond with each other and their boiling points are low. Their oxygen lone pairs can accept H-bonds from water, which makes them soluble."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t05_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A matches 111.7° (III), B matches 141 pm (I), C matches sp3 (II), and D matches phenol + CH3I (IV)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t05_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is true. B is false because ethers are polar (the bent shape leaves a net dipole). C is true because lone-pair delocalisation shortens Ar–O. D is false because the O–CH3 bond breaks. E is true. The correct set is A, C and E."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t06_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "The carbonyl carbon forms three sigma bonds and one pi bond. That makes it sp2 hybridised and trigonal planar, with bond angles of about 120°."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t06_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Propan-1-ol hydrogen-bonds, so it boils highest. Propanal is polar and has dipole-dipole attractions, so it comes next. Butane is nonpolar and boils lowest."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t06_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "The C=O bond is one sigma and one pi bond. Carbon is electron-poor, so it is the electrophilic site; oxygen is the nucleophilic, Lewis-basic site. Carbonyl compounds have larger dipole moments than ethers."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t06_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Lower aldehydes and ketones are miscible with water because they hydrogen-bond with water. They have no O-H hydrogen, so they cannot hydrogen-bond with each other. A is true and R is false."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u17_t06_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A planar carbonyl group can be attacked from either face, so Statement I is false. The resonance description with a dipolar form is correct, so Statement II is true."
    },
